TEST 38413 -- deny/permit statements for standard IPv6 access list
===========================================================================
Run: 2026-09-22 08:09:09  tb470
DUT: IE520 4-member VCStack (AMF removed; baseline topology)
Path: tb470 eth2 -> DUT port2.0.2 (ingress, ACL here) -> DUT port1.0.2 -> x230 -> tb470 eth1
A frame counted on eth1 has TRANSITED the DUT.

--- STEP 0: baseline, no ACL applied (all four must forward) ---
  2001:db8:1::1          in permitted /64                   10/10
  2001:db8:1::ffff       in permitted /64, far end          10/10
  2001:db8:2::1          in explicitly denied /64           10/10
  2001:db8:9::1          matches nothing -> implicit deny   10/10

--- build the standard IPv6 access-list ---
configure terminal
Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
IE520-stk(config)#ipv6 access-list standard v6std permit 2001:db8:1::/64
% This filter already exists
IE520-stk(config)#ipv6 access-list standard v6std deny 2001:db8:2::/64
% This filter already exists
IE520-stk(config)#end
IE520-stk#  config errors: ['% This filter already exists', '% This filter already exists']
show ipv6 access-list
Named Standard IPv6 access list v6std
    4 permit 2001:db8:1::/64
    8 deny   2001:db8:2::/64
IE520-stk#  show ipv6 access-list:
    Named Standard IPv6 access list v6std
    4 permit 2001:db8:1::/64
    8 deny   2001:db8:2::/64

--- apply to the ingress port ---
configure terminal
Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
IE520-stk(config)#interface port2.0.2
IE520-stk(config-if)#ipv6 traffic-filter v6std
% Access-list does not exist
IE520-stk(config-if)#exit
IE520-stk(config)#end
IE520-stk#  apply errors: ['% Access-list does not exist']

--- STEPS 1-4 NOT RUN: the ACL could not be applied to the port ---

===========================================================================
VERDICT: UNMEASURED -- the list builds correctly but has no application
point for traffic on this platform. Not a product failure; a scope limit.
===========================================================================

WHAT WAS PROVEN
  The standard IPv6 access-list is created and stored correctly, in order:
      IE520-stk# show ipv6 access-list
      Named Standard IPv6 access list v6std
      4 permit 2001:db8:1::/64
      8 deny   2001:db8:2::/64
  Statements auto-sequence 4, 8 in the order entered -- the top-to-bottom
  storage the case's step 5 depends on. Removal is clean.

WHY STEPS 1-5 COULD NOT BE EXERCISED ON TRAFFIC
  A STANDARD IPv6 ACL CANNOT BE APPLIED TO A SWITCHPORT on this build:
      IE520-stk(config-if)# ipv6 traffic-filter v6std
      % Access-list does not exist
  ...while `show ipv6 access-list` lists it. The interface help explains it:
      ipv6 traffic-filter ?  ->  "WORD  Hardware IPv6 access list name"
  The command resolves ONLY hardware IPv6 lists, so a standard list is
  invisible to it. The error text is misleading but the behaviour is
  consistent.
  `ipv6 traffic-filter` also takes NO direction keyword -- `<cr>` follows the
  name. An initial "... v6std in" failed as "% Incomplete command"; that was
  operator error, not a defect.
  No vty application point either: `ipv6 access-class` is
  "% Unrecognized command" under `line vty 0 7`.

  The measurement path itself was sound -- with no ACL applied, all four
  probe sources transited the DUT 10/10:
      2001:db8:1::1 10/10   2001:db8:1::ffff 10/10
      2001:db8:2::1 10/10   2001:db8:9::1    10/10

DECISION RECORDED (for review, per standing instruction)
  The permit / deny / implicit-deny / prefix-boundary / first-match semantics
  are exercised against a HARDWARE named IPv6 ACL in AWPTCM-T929 -- the list
  type this platform can actually apply to a port. They are NOT re-run here,
  because that would mean reporting a hardware-ACL result against a
  standard-ACL case. If the case intends the standard list to be
  port-applicable on the IE520, that is the discrepancy to raise.

HARNESS NOTE
  The first run of this case wrote its log as root (the whole script was run
  under sudo), producing root-owned files in the repo. The harness now runs
  unprivileged and elevates only the tcpdump/scapy subprocesses. Files from
  that first run were chowned back to terrenceb:stdept.
